claude-desktop:
In Claude Desktop, open the Claude menu in your system's menu bar (not the settings inside the window), choose Settings, then the Developer tab, then Edit Config, and add this to claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Desktop starts a local program, so replace the command with the full path of Premagentic.McpServer on this computer. Then quit Claude Desktop completely and start it again. Use this local configuration, not a custom connector added in Claude: a custom connector, in claude.ai, Claude Desktop or the mobile apps, is called from Anthropic's cloud and cannot reach a server inside your network.

{
  "mcpServers": {
    "premagentic": {
      "command": "<full path to Premagentic.McpServer>",
      "env": {
        "PREM_API_URL": "{address}",
        "PREM_AGENT_TOKEN": "{token}"
      }
    }
  }
}

chatgpt:
ChatGPT calls a remote MCP server from OpenAI's cloud, so it cannot reach PremAgentic inside your network directly, and it offers no way to send this token as a header.

OpenAI documents Secure MCP Tunnel: a program you run inside your network, which connects out to OpenAI and can start a local MCP program. PremAgentic's bridge, Premagentic.McpServer, is such a program, and it holds this token. This has not been tried with PremAgentic. Everything ChatGPT reads through it reaches OpenAI, so register this assistant with a hosted model.

The bridge, for a person who tries it: the program is Premagentic.McpServer, with these two settings in its environment.

PREM_API_URL={address}
PREM_AGENT_TOKEN={token}

copilot:
In VS Code, add this to .vscode/mcp.json in your workspace, or run MCP: Open User Configuration to add it for every workspace, then start the server from the MCP view.

{
  "servers": {
    "premagentic": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "{address}/mcp",
      "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer {token}" }
    }
  }
}

VS Code's documentation prefers an input variable to a token written into the file: put ${input:premagentic-token} in place of the token, add an "inputs" entry with "type": "promptString", "id": "premagentic-token" and "password": true, and VS Code asks for the token once and stores it.

local-mcp:
For a client that starts a local program, run the PremAgentic bridge, Premagentic.McpServer. It holds no database credentials; it talks to this server with the token. Replace the command with where the bridge is on this computer.

{
  "mcpServers": {
    "premagentic": {
      "command": "<path to Premagentic.McpServer>",
      "env": {
        "PREM_API_URL": "{address}",
        "PREM_AGENT_TOKEN": "{token}"
      }
    }
  }
}
